1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:05,260
Six fifty-two a.m. Your watch starts
counting down. You didn't set it.

2
00:00:06,460 --> 00:00:11,456
Eight minutes and twenty seconds. The
kettle is on. Your little sister Mira is

3
00:00:11,456 --> 00:00:16,645
drawing at the table, and Biscuit is staring
at her toast like it owes him money.

4
00:00:16,995 --> 00:00:19,355
MIRA: Biscuit. You already had breakfast.

5
00:00:19,705 --> 00:00:21,270
He disagrees.

6
00:00:22,070 --> 00:00:27,363
Outside, it is the most ordinary morning of
the year. Twenty-two degrees. Not a

7
00:00:27,363 --> 00:00:30,780
single cloud. Nobody out there
knows what you know.

8
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,420
Three. Two. One.

9
00:00:36,720 --> 00:00:38,405
And the Sun goes out.

10
00:00:40,405 --> 00:00:44,115
No flash. No sound. Just
stars... at breakfast.

11
00:00:50,915 --> 00:00:55,628
Here is the part that should scare you.
Sunlight takes eight minutes and twenty

12
00:00:55,628 --> 00:00:57,000
seconds to reach Earth.

13
00:00:57,350 --> 00:01:02,489
So the Sun did not go out just now. It went
out eight minutes ago... at the exact

14
00:01:02,489 --> 00:01:04,710
second your watch started counting.

15
00:01:06,110 --> 00:01:11,692
One second later, the Moon goes dark too. It
never had any light of its own. It was

16
00:01:11,692 --> 00:01:12,970
only ever a mirror.

17
00:01:13,770 --> 00:01:19,069
And with nothing left to pull it around,
Earth stops curving. It flies off in a

18
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:22,355
straight line, at thirty
kilometres every second.

19
00:01:23,155 --> 00:01:28,327
Everyone is in the street, staring up. Nobody
can get a phone call through, because

20
00:01:28,327 --> 00:01:32,065
everyone on Earth is calling
everyone else at the same time.

21
00:01:32,865 --> 00:01:38,100
Here is the strange thing. It is still
twenty-two degrees. Nothing feels different.

22
00:01:38,450 --> 00:01:43,446
That's the trap. Your first problem isn't the
dark. It's what the dark is about to

23
00:01:43,446 --> 00:01:44,360
do to the heat.

24
00:01:47,760 --> 00:01:53,245
So you pick one room. The smallest one, with
the fewest windows. Every blanket you

25
00:01:53,245 --> 00:01:57,995
own goes over the glass, because a
window leaks heat like an open door.

26
00:01:58,795 --> 00:02:04,444
A resting human gives off about a hundred
watts of heat. You, your sister and a dog?

27
00:02:04,444 --> 00:02:07,605
That is a small heater that
never switches off.

28
00:02:07,955 --> 00:02:09,665
MIRA: Biscuit is the warmest.

29
00:02:10,015 --> 00:02:12,325
Biscuit is also the smelliest.

30
00:02:13,125 --> 00:02:18,932
Second problem: water. When pipes freeze, the
ice inside them grows by nine percent,

31
00:02:18,932 --> 00:02:20,660
and the pipes split open.

32
00:02:21,010 --> 00:02:26,547
So before that happens, you fill the bathtub.
Every pot. Every bottle. Two hundred

33
00:02:26,547 --> 00:02:29,720
litres. If you are careful,
that is four weeks.

34
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:37,937
On the third night, the sky turns green. For
one second, you think it is coming

35
00:02:37,937 --> 00:02:38,255
back.

36
00:02:38,605 --> 00:02:44,002
It isn't. These are particles the Sun threw
out before it died, still crossing

37
00:02:44,002 --> 00:02:47,115
space, still arriving. The
Sun's last letter.

38
00:02:49,515 --> 00:02:52,225
MIRA: I'm drawing it so it
remembers where we live.

39
00:02:52,575 --> 00:02:55,015
She will draw one every single day.

40
00:02:55,815 --> 00:03:00,350
That night, every radio station on
Earth is static. Except one.

41
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,690
(RADIO) Go down. Go down.

42
00:03:03,540 --> 00:03:07,925
Two words, over and over. No
name. No explanation.

43
00:03:11,125 --> 00:03:16,549
Day seven. The average temperature on Earth
is minus eighteen. Every heater in the

44
00:03:16,549 --> 00:03:21,908
city is running at once, and the power grid
cannot take it. You watch the city go

45
00:03:21,908 --> 00:03:23,760
dark... one block at a time.

46
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:31,005
Good news: the whole world is now a freezer.
Your food will never go off. Bad news:

47
00:03:31,005 --> 00:03:33,170
there are eleven days of it left.

48
00:03:33,970 --> 00:03:39,334
You want to light a fire inside. Don't. In
a sealed room, a flame makes carbon

49
00:03:39,334 --> 00:03:44,080
monoxide. You can't see it. You can't
smell it. You just fall asleep.

50
00:03:44,880 --> 00:03:50,514
Then someone opens the front door downstairs.
And Biscuit bolts. Straight out, into

51
00:03:50,514 --> 00:03:52,415
the dark, into minus twenty.

52
00:03:52,765 --> 00:03:53,875
MIRA: Biscuit!

53
00:03:54,225 --> 00:03:56,960
You grab the torch, and
you go after him...

54
00:03:57,760 --> 00:04:03,849
Your breath freezes in the air. No cars. No
birds. No wind. Just silence. Then,

55
00:04:03,849 --> 00:04:07,395
footprints in the snow.
Small ones. Four legs.

56
00:04:08,195 --> 00:04:13,817
They lead to the metro station. And out of the
stairs... warm air is rising. Biscuit

57
00:04:13,817 --> 00:04:17,030
found the one thing nobody
else was looking for.

58
00:04:18,630 --> 00:04:24,374
Underground, the ground still remembers last
summer. And deeper still, the rock gets

59
00:04:24,374 --> 00:04:29,913
warmer, about twenty-five degrees for every
kilometre. Earth has its own heat. It

60
00:04:29,913 --> 00:04:31,965
never needed the Sun for that.

61
00:04:32,765 --> 00:04:38,065
The radio said go down. So you go down. And
you start to wonder how the voice on

62
00:04:38,065 --> 00:04:39,125
that radio knew.

63
00:04:42,325 --> 00:04:47,525
Day thirty. Ten thousand people live in
the tunnels now. Train carriages are

64
00:04:47,525 --> 00:04:53,135
bedrooms. The platforms are streets. Someone
has hung up lights shaped like stars.

65
00:04:53,935 --> 00:04:59,380
Biscuit is now the most popular resident of
the underground. He has eleven new best

66
00:04:59,380 --> 00:05:02,595
friends, and every single one
of them has snacks.

67
00:05:03,395 --> 00:05:08,710
Without the Sun, your body forgets what
time it is. So at seven every morning,

68
00:05:08,710 --> 00:05:14,230
someone turns every lamp up to full, and the
whole tunnel pretends it is sunrise.

69
00:05:15,030 --> 00:05:20,888
Above you, the sea is freezing from the top
down. But ice floats, so it works like a

70
00:05:20,888 --> 00:05:25,490
lid. Under it, the ocean stays
liquid, for a very, very long time.

71
00:05:26,290 --> 00:05:28,025
MIRA: Are we going to run out of air?

72
00:05:28,375 --> 00:05:33,843
No. The plants stopped making oxygen on day
one. But the air already holds enough

73
00:05:33,843 --> 00:05:39,310
for every human alive to breathe for
thousands of years. Air was never the clock.

74
00:05:40,110 --> 00:05:45,781
Food is. Two thousand calories, per person,
per day. The tunnels have ninety days of

75
00:05:45,781 --> 00:05:51,115
it. And a ticket hall full of grow lights,
running on the one power plant still

76
00:05:51,115 --> 00:05:51,520
alive.

77
00:05:54,720 --> 00:06:00,227
Day one hundred. Earth has now drifted
farther out than Mars. No human being has

78
00:06:00,227 --> 00:06:02,430
ever been this far from the Sun.

79
00:06:04,030 --> 00:06:08,490
Then the lights go out. All of them. The
grow lights. The heaters. The pumps.

80
00:06:08,840 --> 00:06:14,230
In the dark, you hear ten thousand people stop
talking at the same time. The tunnels

81
00:06:14,230 --> 00:06:18,530
start losing a degree every day... and
there is nowhere left to go.

82
00:06:19,330 --> 00:06:24,513
Eleven days later, the engineers bring the
plant back. People cheer like it is New

83
00:06:24,513 --> 00:06:29,507
Year. By day one hundred and eighty, there
are a hundred and eighty suns on the

84
00:06:29,507 --> 00:06:30,265
tunnel wall.

85
00:06:31,065 --> 00:06:36,538
And on the radio, there is good news. Four
kilometres under the ice, at the bottom

86
00:06:36,538 --> 00:06:42,011
of the ocean, there are cracks where the
Earth breathes out heat. And around them,

87
00:06:42,011 --> 00:06:47,550
life. Worms, crabs, bacteria. Life that never
needed the Sun. Not for a single day.

88
00:06:49,350 --> 00:06:54,554
Underneath the broadcast, quieter, the other
voice is still there. But tonight, it

89
00:06:54,554 --> 00:06:55,760
says something new.

90
00:06:56,110 --> 00:06:57,275
(RADIO) Look up.

91
00:07:01,275 --> 00:07:07,231
Day three hundred and sixty-five. One year.
Three hundred and sixty-five suns on the

92
00:07:07,231 --> 00:07:07,585
wall.

93
00:07:09,585 --> 00:07:14,962
You climb up to the surface. Minus
seventy-three degrees. Your eyelashes freeze with

94
00:07:14,962 --> 00:07:17,395
a single breath. And then you look up.

95
00:07:18,795 --> 00:07:25,265
No Sun. No city lights. No haze. Just the most
perfect sky any human being has ever

96
00:07:25,265 --> 00:07:25,655
seen.

97
00:07:28,655 --> 00:07:34,106
Earth is out past the orbit of Jupiter now,
carrying everyone you have ever met into

98
00:07:34,106 --> 00:07:34,690
the dark.

99
00:07:36,290 --> 00:07:41,569
And the Moon is still with you. You can't see
it. But you can see where it isn't. A

100
00:07:41,569 --> 00:07:44,050
perfect black circle, eating the stars.

101
00:07:46,050 --> 00:07:49,560
Then your watch beeps. Eight
minutes and twenty seconds.

102
00:07:52,760 --> 00:07:58,893
Six fifty-two a.m. The kettle is on. Sunlight
on the table. Mira is drawing. Biscuit

103
00:07:58,893 --> 00:08:00,645
is staring at her toast.

104
00:08:00,995 --> 00:08:03,835
Nobody remembers. Nobody but you.

105
00:08:04,635 --> 00:08:09,629
Except, on the window, in the middle of
summer... there is frost. In the shape of a

106
00:08:09,629 --> 00:08:09,870
sun.

107
00:08:11,870 --> 00:08:17,086
And your watch is counting again.
Seventy-one hours. Fifty-nine minutes. This

108
00:08:17,086 --> 00:08:18,780
time... it isn't the Sun.

109
00:08:20,180 --> 00:08:24,801
So. If your watch started counting down
right now... what's the first thing you

110
00:08:24,801 --> 00:08:26,790
would do? Tell us in the comments.

111
00:08:27,140 --> 00:08:31,125
And subscribe. Because next time,
the countdown is shorter.
